Nick Szabo asks:

    "According to _Mark Skousen's Complete Guide to
    Financial Privacy_, there are several offshore credit
    card companies that will bill in the currency of your
    choice.   Does anybody have more specifics on this, and
    if such cards are accepted in the U.S.?"

Yup.  See my article in issue #9 of MONDO 2000 ("Dirty Little
Secrets:  Shiny-Brite Swiss Bank Accounts" pg. 124)  Currently,
the Visa card offered by the TSB Bank, in the Channel Island,
seems to be one of the best ideas.  Any foreign ATM, credit or
debit card with a PLUS or CIRRUS logo on the back can be used to
withdraw money from corresponding ATMs in the U.S.  The money
withdrawn is U.S. dollars.  An equivalent amount is withdrawn
from your account in whatever currency it is denominated in.  The
conversion is made at the very favorable interbank rate.

Even under the current monetary structure, electronic fund
transfers create an implicit competition among currencies.  If a
merchant prefers Deutchmarks, credit card payments will be
deposited in his German bank account.  There, they will be
converted to the mark, no matter what currency the card holder
keeps his account denominated in.

Gresham's Law, to the extent it is relevant, would only come into
play in the forex currency conversions are settled among the
merchants and consumers' banks.  At the consumer level, Gresham's
Law would be irrelevant.

