I've been thinking about this.  If a string is incompressible, its
elements are "random", uniformly and independently distributed among
the possible string elements.  Therefore if some math can define such
an incompressible string, some part of math is random.  Fine.

But can't a similar argument conclude "X is random", for any X?
Anything that can be described can be described with a string, and any
string can be described by an incompressible string, which will have
random elements.  Therefore anything describable is random.  QED. 

